gh issue comment 20 -R paulzierep/metagenomic-binning-benchmark --body "## Concrete next optimization ideas (from code review + measured runs)

What has already landed (fix batches on \`comebin-optimizations-v11\`): sequential bin numbering, float64/welford coverage depth, sklearn KMeans n_jobs removal, DataLoader drop_last edge case, seeds. Measured effect so far: fix_v11 on the demo = 71 bins vs 60 baseline at equal wall time; medium/human/marine/CAMI III all run at seed 42.

Ranking for the **next** batch, by expected time×RAM payoff (will be verified on the medium sweep, issue #25):

1. **Leiden grid pruning** — clustering runs 120 sequential Leiden jobs (8×5×3 grid) then re-runs 24 selected seeds. We already skip the unseeded pool. Next: cap the grid with the seeded strategy first, and only expand when candidates are within ~1 pt F1. Could cut 40–50 % of the clustering stage.
2. **gen_kmer.py vectorization** — currently pure-Python \`itertools.tee\` sliding window (vectorized np.char/rolling would cut the data-aug pass substantially on the 1.1 Gbp CAMI III set).
3. **gen_cov.py closed-form covariance** — it materializes per-base depth lists per pair; a streaming Welford pair sum avoids O(contigs × depth) intermediate arrays (biggest RAM saver on marine/CAMI III).
4. **cluster.py set-based membership** — O(n·m) \`in\` scans on arrays → set/interval lookups; matters when contig counts are high (marine 41,988 / cami3 5,000).
5. **Early-stop tuning** — demo/marine early-stop at 159–184/200 (Top-1 > 99 %) saved hours with no accuracy loss; a tighter threshold (e.g. 98 % × 2 epochs) may cut the CAMI III run's full 200 epochs.

All get validated small → medium (docs/09 gate), and only survivors reach CAMI II/III human (issue #25 follow-on). Details: docs/07 + docs/13." 2>&1 | tail -1

